    "englishTranslation": "Vindication of the Probans 'Cognizability' (Dṛśyatva — Part 1): The opponent argues that 'cognizability' (dṛśyatva)—the very logical reason (hetu) used to infer cosmic falsity—cannot be cogently defined. Does it mean 'pervasion by mental modification' (vṛtti-vyāpyatva), 'pervasion by the illuminating result' (phala-vyāpyatva), or 'being an object of consciousness'? In the first case, Brahman itself is pervaded by the final Upaniṣadic mental modification, causing over-extension (vyabhicāra) to Brahman! In the second, remote or subtle objects not revealed by sensory phala would lack the hetu. Madhusūdana replies: Pure, unconditioned Consciousness is self-luminous (svataḥ-sphuraṇa) and never depends on any external mental modification or cognitive fruit for its manifestation; even in liberating realization, the Upaniṣadic vṛtti only removes the veil of Avidyā over conditioned consciousness without turning Pure Brahman into an inert object of phala-vyāpti.",
